TY  - THES
ID  - oai:DiVA.org:kth-367949
A1  - Ljunggren, Tobias
TI  - AI-Driven Metric Extraction of Sustainability Data
PB  - KTH
PY  - 2025
AB  - &#60;p&#62;Corporate sustainability reports provide essential
      information regarding a company’s environmental impact, yet their
      inconsistent formatting and lack of structure pose significant
      challenges for automated Information Extraction (IE). This thesis
      presents an end-to-end IE pipeline leveraging large language models
      (LLMs) to extract quantitative sustainability metrics, such as
      greenhouse gas emissions and energy use, from unstructured PDF
      reports. The system integrates document parsing,
      image-summarization, relevance classification, Retrieval-Augmented
      Generation (RAG), In-Context Learning (ICL), Constrained Decoding
      Generation (CDG), and rule-based validation. A benchmark dataset of
      63 reports was compiled, with a subset used to construct one-shot
      examples. Evaluation against a manually curated gold dataset
      revealed that the system outperforms manual processing in speed but
      falls short of expert-level accuracy. The highest-performing
      extraction method achieved a precision of 48.4%, a recall of 18.0%,
      and an F1 score of 26.3%. A modified MAP evaluation revealed that
      using fine-tuned BERT models to classify related information could
      slightly increase retrieval performance, but it decreased overall
      accuracy. These results reflect the difficulty of processing
      multimodal content and the limitations of semantic search in
      retrieving the correct context within documents where multiple
      sections discuss similar topics using overlapping terminology.
      However, statistical validation using McNemar’s test demonstrated
      that applying rule-based validation significantly improved overall
      extraction accuracy, confirming the value of structured validation
      in LLM-based workflows. Future work should explore improved
      retrieval methods, better handling of multimodal content such as
      image-based tables, and opportunities for human-AI collaboration.
      Enhancements like fine-tuned embedding models, structured
      intermediate outputs, and real-time user feedback could increase
      accuracy and usability.&#60;/p&#62;%%%%&#60;p&#62;Företags
      hållbarhetsrapporter tillhandahåller viktig information om ett
      företags miljöpåverkan, men deras inkonsekventa formatering och
      brist på struktur utgör betydande utmaningar för automatiserad
      informationsextraktion (IE). Denna avhandling presenterar en
      IE-pipeline som använder stora språkmodeller (LLM) för att
      extrahera kvantitativa hållbarhetsmått, såsom
      växthusgasutsläpp och energianvändning, från ostrukturerade
      PDF-rapporter. Systemet integrerar dokumentparsning,
      bildsammanfattning, relevansklassificering, Retrieval-Augmented
      Generation (RAG), In-Context Learning (ICL), Constrained Decoding
      Generation (CDG) och regelbaserad validering. Ett dataset
      bestående av 63 rapporter sammanställdes, där en delmängd
      användes för att konstruera one-shot-exempel. Utvärdering mot en
      manuellt kurerad gulddataset visade att systemet överträffar
      manuell bearbetning i hastighet men inte når upp till
      expertnivåns noggrannhet. Den bäst presterande extraktionsmetoden
      uppnådde en precision på 48,4%, en recall på 18,0% och ett
      F1-värde på 26,3%. En&#8230;
