TY  - THES
ID  - handle:10477/90353
A1  - Chen, Jian
TI  - Guidance Conditions in Generative Modeling: Elevating Discriminative Capabilities and Controllability
PB  - University at Buffalo
PY  - 2025
AB  - Generative models, such as diffusion models and large language
      models (LLMs), play a crucial role in artificial intelligence due
      to their remarkable generative quality and versatility. These
      models leverage deep neural network architectures to sample new
      data points from complex data distributions that cannot be
      expressed in analytical form. Due to the demand for controllability
      in real-world applications, various guidance methods have emerged
      for conditional generation. For example, text-to-image generation
      and image captioning utilize encoded features as conditions, while
      in-context learning conditions generation directly on the input
      data. Achieving this level of conditional adherence requires a
      delicate balance between generative diversity and discriminative
      precision. My research focuses on leveraging generative models for
      generative tasks that require deterministic precision or
      discriminative tasks under uncertainty. The first section of this
      thesis introduces the development of the LRA-diffusion model, which
      is a stochastic classifier trained using noisy labels. This work
      formulates classification as an image-guided label generation task.
      It learns from noisy labels and generates label estimation based on
      learned label distribution for images by leveraging neighbor
      consistency in the feature space of pre-trained models, such as
      SimCLR and CLIP. The second section presents the development of
      LACE, a diffusion model for layout generation, which unifies
      various conditional layout generation tasks in a single model by
      masking partial attributes in training. By training diffusion
      models with differentiable constraints, LACE achieves superior
      performance across several metrics, including FID, MaxIoU, and
      Alignment, compared to existing discrete diffusion models. With the
      advent of large language models (LLMs) and their ability to
      understand and generate structured text, we explore how text
      guidance and content awareness can further enhance controlled
      generation in layout tasks. The third section introduces TextLap,
      an LLM customized for layout generation, where generation is
      explicitly conditioned on text prompts. By supervised fine-tuning
      on curated datasets, TextLap learns to arrange spatial
      relationships between layout elements and generate structured
      representations, such as CSS and JSON. The final section focuses on
      enhancing efficiency and accuracy in multi-modal large language
      models (MLLMs) through refined multimodal conditioning. This work
      introduces a self-visual-RAG framework that enables MLLMs to
      process multi-page, visually rich documents efficiently. It
      optimizes MLLMs as multimodal retrievers, reducing the input length
      by retrieving relevant pages from the document, allowing MLLMs to
      focus on a shorter context before generating responses. This
      structured approach refines multimodal conditions, ensuring that
      text generation remains accurate, contextually grounded, and
      computationally efficient.%%%%**To request an accessible version of
      the file(s) associated with this item, contact
      library@buffalo.edu.&#8230;
